BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 728.0 Td (  In the city of Aethelgard, the Weaver held a spool of silver thread given by the gods. He coul) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 718.0 Td (    d keep the silver, steady and cold, or offer it to the Merchant who tended the Great Loom. T) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (    he Loom was a miraculous engine; it transformed a single strand of silver into three of gold) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (    , but only if the trust between the two was absolute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (  The Weaver stood before the Merchant. "If I give you all," the Weaver whispered, "our wealth t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (    riples, but the choice to share the harvest rests solely in your hands.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (  The Merchant took the silver, his eyes reflecting the potential of the machine. He knew that t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (    o withhold the gold was to invite a curse upon his house; to hoard was to turn the gold back) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (     into lead. He returned half the harvest, a gesture that bound them in a pact of prosperity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (  They learned that greed is a shallow well, but reciprocity is a flowing river. Each round, the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (    y returned to the Loom, trading silver for gold, trust for return. By the end, they were no ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (    longer mere partners, but architects of an empire built on the bedrock of a shared promise: ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (    that one plus one, when held in faith, becomes three.) Tj ET
