BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 448.0 Td (      In the city of Aethelgard, the Weaver held a spool of silver thread given by the gods. He ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 438.0 Td (        could keep the silver, steady and cold, or offer it to the Merchant who tended the Great) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 428.0 Td (         Loom. The Loom was a miraculous engine; it transformed a single strand of silver into t) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 418.0 Td (        hree of gold, but only if the trust between the two was absolute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 408.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 398.0 Td (      The Weaver stood before the Merchant. "If I give you all," the Weaver whispered, "our weal) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 388.0 Td (        th triples, but the choice to share the harvest rests solely in your hands.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (      The Merchant took the silver, his eyes reflecting the potential of the machine. He knew th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (        at to withhold the gold was to invite a curse upon his house; to hoard was to turn the g) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (        old back into lead. He returned half the harvest, a gesture that bound them in a pact of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (         prosperity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (      They learned that greed is a shallow well, but reciprocity is a flowing river. Each round,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (         they returned to the Loom, trading silver for gold, trust for return. By the end, they ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (        were no longer mere partners, but architects of an empire built on the bedrock of a shar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (        ed promise: that one plus one, when held in faith, becomes three.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (    reasoning:) Tj ET
