BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      In the beginning, the Weaver and the Wanderer stood before the Golden Orchard. The Wandere) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (        r held five seeds of starlight, a limited treasury of hope. To sow them in the Weaver's ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (        soil was a gamble, for the earth would triple whatever was planted. If the Wanderer kept) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (         the seeds, their pockets remained heavy but the soil remained barren. If the Wanderer g) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (        ave, the Weaver could nurture the growth, returning a portion or hoarding the harvest.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (      Trust was the invisible rain. When the Wanderer cast their seeds, they looked to the Weave) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (        r, not for a guarantee, but for an echo of their own generosity. The Weaver, sensing the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (         vulnerability, understood that the orchard's abundance depended on the cycle of return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (         If the Weaver returned only chaff, the Wanderer would cease to sow, and the cycle would) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (         die in silence. But when both recognized the multiplier of communal growth, the orchard) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (         bloomed in brilliant hues, yielding more than either could have coaxed from the earth a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (        lone. They learned that the greatest harvest was not the grain itself, but the enduring ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (        bridge built between two souls who dared to trust the unseen harvest.) Tj ET
